.TH TAIL 1 "October 8, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
tail \- deliver the last part of a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-n " number" | \-number | \-c " number" ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tail
copies the last part of the named file to the standard output.
If no file is named, the standard input is used.
The default is the last ten lines.
.TP
.B \-n number
Copy the last number lines. The spelling -number is also accepted.
.TP
.B \-c number
Copy the last number bytes.
.TP
.B \-f
After copying, do not stop: look at the file once a second and copy
whatever has been appended, until interrupted (a typed ^C ends it,
status 130). The file is reopened on every look, because a ProDOS
file's end-of-file is not shared between two openers; a file that has
become shorter is followed from its start. Needs a timer (a mouse card
or a Mockingboard) and refuses without one, like sleep(1). Ignored
when reading the standard input.
.PP
A final unterminated line is counted as a line.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/TAIL  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
head(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 if the input cannot be opened, 2 for usage or range errors.
.SH BUGS
Tails relative to the end of the file are treasured up in a buffer, and thus are limited in length. Here the limits are 64 lines, 255 bytes per stored line, and 255 bytes for -c. There is no +number start-relative form. With -f the wait between looks is a polling loop, so it holds the processor as sleep(1) does.
